(6757461, 1010741, 96721, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :) <br/> <br/>Composition: <br/> <br/>Love the scene and overall composition, the angle is great and really adds to the theme.<br/> <br/>Technicals: <br/> <br/>Are good I''m assuming the flat looking foreground is down to the flare from the lights so there''s nothing that could be done about that and obviously the whole would not have the "emergency" feel that it achieves without the light being on and the flare adds to that feeling.<br/> <br/>Post-Processing: <br/> <br/>Is pretty good I think it could have been improved a little by extending the lens flare over the T-Rex as he is behind the flare, also a little noise reduction on the foreground and then some selective sharpening an some burning might have brought out the foreground and balanced the overall image a bit - also as I said in my message during voting I would have loved to see someone added alongside the ambulance running past it with their arms flailing I really think this could have made an even better picture and who knows maybe even got you your first ribbon.<br/> <br/>My Opinion: <br/> <br/>I like the idea of this shot a lot - I was one of your 7s and thought you would place in the top ten which you did so you definitely hit it with the voters so well done. <br/> <br/>Hope this was of some use, should you wish to discuss any part of the critique further please feel free to PM me. <br/> <br/>Good luck in future challenges! <br/> <br/>Mark</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :)  Composition:  Love the scene and overall composition, the angle is great and really adds to the theme. Technicals:  Are good I''m assuming the flat looking foreground is down to the flare from the lights so there''s nothing that could be done about that and obviously the whole would not have the "emergency" feel that it achieves without the light being on and the flare adds to that feeling. Post-Processing:  Is pretty good I think it could have been improved a little by extending the lens flare over the T-Rex as he is behind the flare, also a little noise reduction on the foreground and then some selective sharpening an some burning might have brought out the foreground and balanced the overall image a bit - also as I said in my message during voting I would have loved to see someone added alongside the ambulance running past it with their arms flailing I really think this could have made an even better picture and who knows maybe even got you your first ribbon. My Opinion:  I like the idea of this shot a lot - I was one of your 7s and thought you would place in the top ten which you did so you definitely hit it with the voters so well done.  Hope this was of some use, should you wish to discuss any part of the critique further please feel free to PM me.  Good luck in future challenges!  Mark', '2012-05-02 05:25:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(6757492, 1010927, 102030, '<td valign="top">As Emergency Helicopter Pilot also, I have to say that your photo is amazing and unique. Thanks for sharing.</td>', 'As Emergency Helicopter Pilot also, I have to say that your photo is amazing and unique. Thanks for sharing.', '2012-05-02 07:25:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(6757502, 1010584, 67258, '<td valign="top">Welcome from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>My initial thoughts on viewing this photo is that it is a thoroughly interesting image that is a bit of a stretch for the challenge.<br/> <br/>On a technical level, you have used a challenging camera technique to good effect.  Composition is very good, although some may prefer all of the tiger''s rear leg to be in the shot - I really like the way s/he is leaping out of the corner.<br/> <br/>It is on the aesthetic and emotional level, though, that I think the success of this photo lies.  The image is full of intrigue - the strange figure in white leading or charming the tiger as though he were a pied piper figure, the ghostly people looking on, the only child whose face is visible has a completely dead-pan, inscrutible expression.  Aesthetically I love the way the blur has created a feeling of texture, and has somewhat muted the colours and partially erased all shapes escept the tiger.<br/> <br/>In voting I gave this a 6, pretty much for the reasons I have given in the preceeding paragraph.  I did feel that, to me at any rate, it didn''t depict an emergency; there were two things in particular thta contributed to that idea not coming across - both really nit-picky, but could (possibly) have been countered with use of ''expert'' editing - the tiger''s gaze is not on the figure he is following/leaping at, and one of the people is holding a camera which makes him a straight spectator ata zoo rather than a part of (my imagined) scene.<br/> <br/>I haven''t been particularly articulate in addressing this iage, but I hope you get my drift!  If you want any clarification, please feel free to send me a PM.<br/> <br/>Regards,  Sara.</td>', 'Welcome from the Critique Club. My initial thoughts on viewing this photo is that it is a thoroughly interesting image that is a bit of a stretch for the challenge. On a technical level, you have used a challenging camera technique to good effect.  Composition is very good, although some may prefer all of the tiger''s rear leg to be in the shot - I really like the way s/he is leaping out of the corner. It is on the aesthetic and emotional level, though, that I think the success of this photo lies.  The image is full of intrigue - the strange figure in white leading or charming the tiger as though he were a pied piper figure, the ghostly people looking on, the only child whose face is visible has a completely dead-pan, inscrutible expression.  Aesthetically I love the way the blur has created a feeling of texture, and has somewhat muted the colours and partially erased all shapes escept the tiger. In voting I gave this a 6, pretty much for the reasons I have given in the preceeding paragraph.  I did feel that, to me at any rate, it didn''t depict an emergency; there were two things in particular thta contributed to that idea not coming across - both really nit-picky, but could (possibly) have been countered with use of ''expert'' editing - the tiger''s gaze is not on the figure he is following/leaping at, and one of the people is holding a camera which makes him a straight spectator ata zoo rather than a part of (my imagined) scene. I haven''t been particularly articulate in addressing this iage, but I hope you get my drift!  If you want any clarification, please feel free to send me a PM. Regards,  Sara.', '2012-05-02 07:48:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(6757789, 1010927, 31384, '<td valign="top">WOW.... Simply incredible. Although I look at the top 10 photos in each challenge every week, I haven''t commented on anything in years. Had to for this photo though. Congrats on the excellent score, and more importantly, the excellent photo.<br/> <br/>By the way, the three people who voted a "2" should be perma-banned from DPC. That score is despicable for this photo.</td>', 'WOW.... Simply incredible. Although I look at the top 10 photos in each challenge every week, I haven''t commented on anything in years. Had to for this photo though. Congrats on the excellent score, and more importantly, the excellent photo. By the way, the three people who voted a "2" should be perma-banned from DPC. That score is despicable for this photo.', '2012-05-02 13:29:46.000000', NULL, 0),
